  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 949 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nChilly with scattered snow showers to start as cyclonic N-NE flow\nacross Lake Superior (delta t/s up to 16c) in wake of departing\nlow is supporting lake effect snow showers into far northern WI. \nHRRR still showing embedded more organized bands drifting across \nVilas County tonight, so though most accumulations will be \nlimited to a dusting, a few spots could see up to 0.5 inch of \nsnow. Otherwise, a few sprinkles and flurries could occur into \nFriday morning elsewhere. Showers late tonight into Friday morning \nalong Lake Michigan may get a boost as winds veer more NE and  \nedge of system to east backs in. Surface temps will be warm enough\nto keep ptype mainly rain, but some light snow could mix in for a\ntime on Friday morning. Later Friday, increasing subsidence and \nlowering inversions will spell end to most lake effect over the \nnorth and showers along Lake Michigan will also come to an end. \nSoundings and RRFS cloud projections point to scattered to broken \ncu persisting well into the afternoon. Suppose there could be a \nsprinkle or flurry over the north from these clouds. Skies then \nclear out on Friday night as high pressure moves across. Gusty \nwinds from earlier today will slowly diminish into Friday \nafternoon. Winds could still be gusty over eastern WI into Friday \nmorning though as the pressure gradient only slowly relaxes.\n\nSaturday into next week...High pressure will bring dry weather \nover the state into early next week, with another warming trend \nbeginning Sunday. Peak of warmth is Monday with a 40-60% of \nexceeding 60 degrees south of highway 29. Average highs are in the\nmid to upper 40s. Tranquil conditions will last until the middle \nof next week. There are differences in strength of the upper \ntrough forecast to drop across the region by that time, but \nensemble blend (LREF versus the NBM) indicates some chances of \nshowers Tuesday night into later next week. It also could turn \ncool enough again for light snow to return over the north. In \ngeneral though the pattern looks to remain warm and dry into the \nmiddle of the month.",
